// inscne.r336 — grammatical gender as a lenition trigger (§40).
//
// §36 rejected gender for carrying meaning (distinguishing possessors),
// and that stands. This is a different question: gender here carries no
// meaning at all, it only requires form — the adjective after a feminine
// noun lenites, same fact type as tá/bhfuil (§14) or mura/murab (§31): one
// word, two shapes, chosen by position, not meaning.
//
// Word order: Irish adjectives follow their noun ("fear mór", "bean
// mhór"). Pre-0.11 the language wrote the adjective first, which was
// English word order in Irish words — fixed the same way §31.4 fixed the
// copula.

// TYPE NAMES. A type name has gender too, but nothing else in the grammar
// puts an adjective after one, so it must be stated explicitly — and the
// declaration checks itself, since it's an adjective agreeing with its own
// noun (E526 if it doesn't).
//
// Optional, deliberately: nothing yet demands agreement with a type's
// gender, so requiring the declaration would be ceremony.
struchtúr Duine firinscneach {
    ainm: Teaghrán
}

struchtúr Aois bhaininscneach {
    luach: Uimhir
}

// BINDINGS. No new syntax: every binding already carries an adjective
// (seasmhach/sealadach), and its form IS the gender declaration.
duine seasmhach = Duine { ainm: "Cáit" }
aois sheasmhach = Aois { luach: 20 }

// Same for sealadach, same adjective in both forms.
comhaireamh sealadach: Uimhir = 0
tuairisc shealadach: Teaghrán = ""

// x isn't an Irish noun at all, so it defaults to masculine — not an
// exception, just the rule applied to a word with no gender slot (§5).
x seasmhach = 3

feidhm cur(a: Uimhir, b: Uimhir) -> Uimhir { a + b }

gníomh príomh() {
    cuir cur(comhaireamh, luach ó aois) ar chomhaireamh
    cuir ainm ó dhuine ar thuairisc
    scríobh tuairisc
    scríobh comhaireamh
    scríobh x
}